 "drivingQuestion": "How can the bull's head face south and its face west, and why not stand it straight?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "Rav explains the posture; Abaye explains why it is not stood straight",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "yoma.36a.6",
   "daf": "36a",
   "sentenceId": 5,
   "he": "רֹאשׁוֹ לַדָּרוֹם וּפָנָיו לַמַּעֲרָב. הֵיכִי מַשְׁכַּחַתְּ לַהּ? אָמַר רַב: בְּעוֹקֵם אֶת רֹאשׁוֹ. וְנוֹקְמֵיהּ לְהֶדְיָא. אָמַר אַבָּיֵי: גְּזֵירָה שֶׁמָּא יַרְבִּיץ גְּלָלִים.",
   "en": "§ The mishna continues: «The head» of the bull was facing «to the south and its face» was facing «to the west.» The Gemara asks: Under «what circumstances» can a case «be found» where its head is toward one direction and its face is toward another? «Rav said:» It is a case where the animal is standing north-south and «it turns its head» and faces west. The Gemara asks: «And let us stand it straight» east-west with its back to the altar and its head facing the Sanctuary. «Abaye said: It is» prohibited due to «a decree lest» the bull «defecate» opposite the altar, which is a display of contempt for the altar."
  }
 ],
